\section{Binary Search Trees}

\begin{note}
A binary search tree node has three fields: a \lstinline{val}, a \lstinline{left} subtree, and a \lstinline{right} subtree.
Both subtrees are themselves trees, and the empty tree is represented by \lstinline{None}.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
The \emph{BST invariant} says that for every node,
every value in the left subtree is strictly smaller than the node's value,
and every value in the right subtree is strictly larger.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
The BST invariant is what makes search fast,
and it is also what makes an \emph{inorder traversal}
(visit left, then the node, then right)
produce the values in sorted order.
That is the entire reason a tree can be used as a sorted container:
the traversal does the sorting for you.
\end{note}
